**Goal** — Add opt-in narrowing of built-in and provider tool declarations to native sessions without widening profiles or altering default behavior.

**Motivation** — The design rules that apply here: subagents are nested sessions in the same process, run under a mode equal to or narrower than the parent's, get the parent's tools intersected with the agent's list, take their own model through `agent.modelAliases` with `inherit` meaning the parent's model, and have depth 1.

**Approach** — `NativeBackendOptions` gains `allowedTools?: readonly string[]`, validated by the options schema as an array of non-empty strings. Absent means today's behaviour exactly. Present, it narrows what the session declares, in this order:

1. The profile's built-in tools (`declaredToolsFor(profile, protectedPaths)`) are kept only when their name is in `allowedTools`, except the universal scratchpad tools (`UNIVERSAL_CODING_TOOLS`: `ScratchpadWrite`, `ScratchpadRead`, `ScratchpadList`), which are always kept. `buildSessionToolSupport` does this from its new `allowedTools` argument.
2. Provider tools are admitted through `resolveProviderTools`'s existing `admits` option, which `openNative` passes: a tool `<id>__<local>` is admitted when `allowedTools` contains that exact name or contains `<id>__*`.
3. Grants and ask rules are computed from the narrowed built-in list, so a tool that is not declared is never granted, and the ask rules for a declared tool are unchanged.

An `allowedTools` entry naming nothing available is ignored. The narrowing never adds a tool. Embedder tools (`CreateAgentSessionOptions.tools`) are not filtered. The public API dump lists type names only, so it does not change.

**Scope** — In: existing native-backend options/schema, backend wiring, session-tool-support filtering and existing session tests. Out: Claude Code name mapping (US-004), nested session creation (US-007), host/provider composition (US-009). Embedder tools (`CreateAgentSessionOptions.tools`) are not filtered by `allowedTools`.

**Interface** —
```ts
// packages/nax-agent/src/session/native-backend-options.ts
export interface NativeBackendOptions {
  // ...existing fields unchanged...
  /** Narrow the declared tools to these names (built-in names, `<provider>__<tool>`, or `<provider>__*`); scratchpad tools are always kept. */
  readonly allowedTools?: readonly string[];
}

// packages/nax-agent/src/session/session-tool-support.ts
export interface SessionToolSupportArgs {
  // ...existing fields unchanged...
  readonly allowedTools?: readonly string[];
}
```

Extend existing native-backend-options, native-backend-providers, session-tool-support and interaction/profile test harnesses. Assert actual declarations and denied invocation, not just helper output, and show the narrowing regression failing before implementation.

**Goal** — Create a memory-only native subagent session with parent-equivalent settings and a minimal create/send/close skeleton.

**Motivation** — The design rules that apply here: subagents are nested sessions in the same process, run under a mode equal to or narrower than the parent's, get the parent's tools intersected with the agent's list, take their own model through `agent.modelAliases` with `inherit` meaning the parent's model, and have depth 1.

**Approach** — `runSubagent(input, deps = _subagentSessionDeps)` (new, `src/chat/subagent-session.ts`) runs one task to completion.

Session options (US-007): `createAgentSession` with `profile` from `chatSessionSettings(mode)`; `nativeBackend({ model, bashApproval (from the same settings), allowUnsandboxed: true, allowUnsandboxedCommands: true, carryHistoryAcrossModels: true, loopHandlers, loopHandlerRole: "subagent", toolProviders, allowedTools (only when given), catalogOverrides and compaction exactly as open-chat-session.ts passes them from config })`; `workdir: cwd`; `transcriptStore: createMemoryTranscriptStore()` (not persisted, not listed, not resumable); `metadata: { parentSessionId, subagent: agent.qualifiedName }`; `approvalTimeoutMs: config.execution.approvalTimeout`; `instructions: buildSubagentInstructions({ agent, mode, rulesIndex })` where the rules index comes from `resolveRuleSet` called the way `open-chat-session.ts` calls it. US-007 implements a minimal skeleton around these options: create the session, send the task, consume events to `turn_end`, close, and return `{ content: output }`; its tests use a stub session whose turn ends immediately.

**Scope** — In: subagent-session module, dependency seams, rule-index lookup, session/backend option mirroring, failed-option-construction short-circuiting, preservation of explicit empty restrictions, and immediate-completion tests. Out: full status/error/cancellation behavior and rule-delivery role changes (US-008), model/tool resolution (US-004), ask/cost ownership (US-009/010). Nested sessions are not persisted: they use an in-memory transcript store, are not listed by `/sessions`, and cannot be resumed; a resumed chat session does not restore a subagent that was running when the process died. Narrowing a subagent's mode below the chat session's (for example a read-only profile for an agent whose tools are all read-only) is not done; the tool narrowing is the only narrowing.

**Interface** —
```ts
export interface RunSubagentInput {
  readonly agent: AgentResource;
  readonly task: string;
  readonly mode: ChatMode;               // the chat session's mode at call time
  readonly model: string;                // from resolveSubagentModel
  readonly allowedTools?: readonly string[]; // from mapSubagentTools
  readonly toolProviders: readonly ToolProvider[]; // the chat resources' providers, never the agents provider
  readonly loopHandlers: LoopHandlerSet; // the chat host's handler set
  readonly cwd: string;
  readonly repoRoot: string;
  readonly config: NaxConfig;
  readonly parentSessionId: string;
  readonly signal?: AbortSignal;
  readonly onEvent: (event: SessionEvent) => void;
  /** Called with the nested session once it is created, before its turn starts. */
  readonly onSession?: (session: AgentSession) => void;
}

export const _subagentSessionDeps = {
  createAgentSession,
  nativeBackend,
  createMemoryTranscriptStore,
  resolveRuleSet,
};
```

The US-001 -> US-007 seam stubs nativeBackend to capture allowedTools and proves it equals the supplied input, while absent allowedTools remains an absent key. The US-005 -> US-007 seam checks instructions end with the agent prompt. Call resolveRuleSet with repoRoot/packageDir equal to input repoRoot and configured override fields, then map through toRulesIndexEntry exactly like the opener. Use captured backend/session options and a turn_end-immediate stub, not actual LLM calls. The failed-option-construction negative test asserts only that createAgentSession is not reached; error-result formatting remains US-008's responsibility. Treat allowedTools: [] as an explicit restriction, not an absent value. Empty catalogOverrides must be omitted exactly as the existing opener does.

**Goal** — Complete nested turn execution, terminal result mapping, cancellation, guaranteed cleanup and rule delivery for subagent sessions.

**Motivation** — The design rules that apply here: subagents are nested sessions in the same process, run under a mode equal to or narrower than the parent's, get the parent's tools intersected with the agent's list, take their own model through `agent.modelAliases` with `inherit` meaning the parent's model, and have depth 1.

**Approach** — Turn and result (US-008): rule delivery acts for the nested session (`"subagent"` joins `RULE_READ_ROLES` and `RULE_WRITE_ROLES` in `src/context/rules/rule-delivery/index.ts`); it sends `task` as the only message, passes every event to `onEvent` in order, and on `turn_end` returns: status `completed` -> `{ content: output }`, or `(the subagent returned no text)` when `output` is empty; any other status -> `{ isError: true, content: "subagent <qualifiedName> <status>: <error.message, or the status when there is no error>" }`. `signal` aborting calls the nested session's `cancel()`; a signal already aborted at the start returns `{ isError: true, content: "subagent <qualifiedName> cancelled: cancelled" }` without creating a session. The session is closed in every outcome. A throw from `createAgentSession` or `send` returns `{ isError: true, content: "[chat] subagent <qualifiedName> failed: <message>" }`.

**Scope** — In: complete the US-007 runner, extend its tests, add subagent to both delivery role lists and extend existing rule-delivery tests. Out: changing session option semantics (US-007), ownership of asks (US-009), host listener/cost forwarding and host teardown (US-010). Background (non-blocking) subagents are not supported; a `nax-agents__run` call returns when the nested turn ends.

**Interface** — Continue `runSubagent(input, deps = _subagentSessionDeps)` and the existing interface from US-007:
```ts
export interface RunSubagentInput {
  readonly agent: AgentResource;
  readonly task: string;
  readonly mode: ChatMode;               // the chat session's mode at call time
  readonly model: string;                // from resolveSubagentModel
  readonly allowedTools?: readonly string[]; // from mapSubagentTools
  readonly toolProviders: readonly ToolProvider[]; // the chat resources' providers, never the agents provider
  readonly loopHandlers: LoopHandlerSet; // the chat host's handler set
  readonly cwd: string;
  readonly repoRoot: string;
  readonly config: NaxConfig;
  readonly parentSessionId: string;
  readonly signal?: AbortSignal;
  readonly onEvent: (event: SessionEvent) => void;
  /** Called with the nested session once it is created, before its turn starts. */
  readonly onSession?: (session: AgentSession) => void;
}

export const _subagentSessionDeps = {
  createAgentSession,
  nativeBackend,
  createMemoryTranscriptStore,
  resolveRuleSet,
};
```

Call onSession after creation and before send starts so host routing can own synchronous asks. Final text comes from turn_end.output, not text_delta. Use finally-based cleanup and detach abort listeners on every completion; no session exists to close when creation itself fails. Preserve the existing tui delivery role and per-session rule-delivery state. A native send may throw synchronously or during iteration, and both are mapped to the specified ToolResult.

**Goal** — Attach agents to every native chat open path and route nested approvals/questions to their owning session.

**Motivation** — The design rules that apply here: subagents are nested sessions in the same process, run under a mode equal to or narrower than the parent's, get the parent's tools intersected with the agent's list, take their own model through `agent.modelAliases` with `inherit` meaning the parent's model, and have depth 1.

**Approach** — A new `src/chat/subagent-runs.ts` keeps host-side state for running nested sessions so `chat-host.ts` stays small:

`SubagentRunsDeps` gives it `current()` (the chat session's id, mode and model; called only inside `run`, never at construction, because it throws before a session is open), the catalog, `config`, `cwd`, `repoRoot`, the base `toolProviders`, the host's loop handlers, the ask controller, `ledger()` (a getter: the host creates its ledger lazily when the first session opens), and `turnListener()` (the running send's `onEvent` or `undefined`). `run` resolves the model and tools (US-004), calls `runSubagent`, and routes the nested events:

- `approval_requested` and `question`: record `requestId -> nested session`, then hand the ask controller a copy whose `reason` (approval) or `text` (question) is prefixed `[agent <qualifiedName>] `. `approval_resolved` removes the record, and the nested session closing removes all of its records. Full mode's auto-allow and the per-session allow memory apply unchanged, because the controller does both.

Host changes (US-009): `NativeChatHost` creates one `SubagentRuns` and, when `resources.catalog.agents` is non-empty, passes `[...resources.toolProviders, createAgentsProvider({ catalog, run: runs.run })]` at all three open sites (start, resume, reopen for a mode or model switch); with no agents it passes `resources.toolProviders` itself, the same array as today. The ask controller's `answer` closure answers on `runs.sessionFor(requestId)` when that is defined, otherwise on the chat session.

**Scope** — In: SubagentRuns state/API and ask ownership, lazy host dependency getters, model/tool resolution on each run, provider composition at all three open paths, shared host seam harness, and nested ask integration. Out: implement usage/cost/tool forwarding and host cancel/close draining in US-010; implement listing in US-011. The interface includes those US-010 operations now so the next story can complete them without changing the abstraction. Subagent depth is 1: a nested session never receives the `nax-agents` provider, and an agent's `Agent` or `Task` tool entry is dropped. Claude Code agent frontmatter other than `name`, `description`, `model` and `tools` (for example `color`, `permissionMode`, `hooks`, `skills`) is ignored; a subagent always runs in the chat session's current mode.

**Interface** —
```ts
export interface SubagentRuns {
  /** Run one agent task for the chat session; wires asks, usage, cost and tool events. */
  run(agent: AgentResource, task: string, signal?: AbortSignal): Promise<ToolResult>;
  /** The nested session that issued this request id, if one is still running. */
  sessionFor(requestId: string): AgentSession | undefined;
  /** Spend of nested turns since the last call; resets to 0. */
  takeCost(): number;
  cancelAll(): void;
  closeAll(): Promise<void>;
}
export function createSubagentRuns(deps: SubagentRunsDeps): SubagentRuns;
```

Host-level seam ACs (US-009, US-010) use one harness: `createChatHost` with no `open` override, a fixture catalog built by `buildResourceCatalog`, and `_openChatSessionDeps.createAgentSession` stubbed to return a scripted chat session. `_openChatSessionDeps.nativeBackend` is stubbed to capture its options. The scripted session's `send` finds the provider with id `nax-agents` in the captured `toolProviders`, calls its `tools(cwd)`, then calls the `run` tool with `{ agent, task }` and a context carrying the turn's `AbortSignal`, then ends its turn. The nested session is driven through `_subagentSessionDeps` stubs. Share this harness in test/helpers/chat-subagents.ts and use it from host-subagents.test.ts; pure coordinator ask cases belong in subagent-runs.test.ts. Restore every seam after each test.

The alias seam uses reviewer with model sonnet and agent.modelAliases: { sonnet: "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5-5" }, entering at host.send and asserting captured nested backend options. A sibling seam maps tools Read, WebFetch to [Read]. Existing chat-host-resources.test.ts's toBe(resources.toolProviders) must stay unchanged for empty catalogs. Register request ownership before controller.handle because full mode may answer synchronously. Remove all mappings in runner completion cleanup, and never capture a pre-open current state in the constructor.

**Goal** — Attribute nested usage/spend to chat, forward nested tool activity only, and coordinate nested cancellation and teardown.

**Motivation** — The main nax roadmap (user ruling 2026-10-24) makes nax a full interactive coding agent.

**Approach** — Complete the host-side state in `src/chat/subagent-runs.ts` so `chat-host.ts` stays small.

`SubagentRunsDeps` gives it `current()` (the chat session's id, mode and model; called only inside `run`, never at construction, because it throws before a session is open), the catalog, `config`, `cwd`, `repoRoot`, the base `toolProviders`, the host's loop handlers, the ask controller, `ledger()` (a getter: the host creates its ledger lazily when the first session opens), and `turnListener()` (the running send's `onEvent` or `undefined`). `run` resolves the model and tools (US-004), calls `runSubagent`, and routes the nested events:

- `usage`: `ledger.record({ ...event, sessionId: <chat session id> }, previousAt, model, "subagent")`, where `previousAt` is the nested turn's `turn_start.at` or the previous usage's `at`.
- `turn_end`: its `costUsd` is added to the amount `takeCost()` returns.
- `tool_call` and `tool_result`: passed to `turnListener()` when one is set.
- Other nested events are not forwarded.

Ledger and cost (US-010): `ChatLedger.record` gains an optional fourth argument `role: "tui" | "subagent"` (default `tui`) written as the row's `sessionRole`. `RunChatTurnOptions` gains `extraCostUsd?: () => number`, read once after `turn_end`; `writeTurnMeta` adds it to `ended.costUsd`. `host.send` sets the turn listener for the send's duration and passes `extraCostUsd: () => runs.takeCost()`; in a `finally` it clears the listener and calls `runs.takeCost()` once more, so nested spend from a send that threw is dropped rather than added to the next send. `cancel()` also calls `runs.cancelAll()`; `close()` awaits `runs.closeAll()` before the chat session closes.

**Scope** — In: complete nested usage, spend, event forwarding, cancelAll/closeAll; optional ledger/turn-runner extensions; host send/cancel/close wiring and existing shared host seam tests. Out: provider attachment/ask policy changes (US-009), /agents formatting (US-011). The transcript shows a subagent's tool calls and results only; its streamed text and thinking are not shown, and its final text appears as the `nax-agents__run` tool result.

**Interface** —
```ts
export interface SubagentRuns {
  /** Run one agent task for the chat session; wires asks, usage, cost and tool events. */
  run(agent: AgentResource, task: string, signal?: AbortSignal): Promise<ToolResult>;
  /** The nested session that issued this request id, if one is still running. */
  sessionFor(requestId: string): AgentSession | undefined;
  /** Spend of nested turns since the last call; resets to 0. */
  takeCost(): number;
  cancelAll(): void;
  closeAll(): Promise<void>;
}
export function createSubagentRuns(deps: SubagentRunsDeps): SubagentRuns;
```
`ChatLedger.record(event, previousAt, model?, role?: "tui" | "subagent")`; `RunChatTurnOptions` gains `extraCostUsd?: () => number`.

Use the US-009 shared host harness: createChatHost with no open override, real buildResourceCatalog fixture, captured _openChatSessionDeps.nativeBackend options, scripted parent send invoking the actual nax-agents provider run with its turn signal, and nested _subagentSessionDeps stubs. Enter at host.send to prove nested usage reaches the ledger and nested spend reaches host.current().meta.costUsd. Extend existing turn-runner.test.ts for row roles and optional extra cost; existing callers and expectations stay unchanged.

Only turn_end contributes to takeCost; usage rows must not also increment that accumulator. Track previousAt per nested invocation and use the prior usage timestamp after the first usage. Do not forward nested boundaries/text/thinking to the main transcript listener. Maintain host close idempotency and existing operation/lock/resource teardown ordering while awaiting all nested close completions before the parent closes. The chat /cost command already reads metadata and needs no separate accounting mechanism.
